# Never commit the Pinecone key or pulled catalogue data
.pinecone_key
.gemini_key
.supabase_key
hitech_sample.json
hitech_manifest.json
rebuild.log
done_pages.txt

# Source documents and ingest state. The research PDFs carry unit economics,
# margins, supplier lists and investment figures -- publishing those to a git
# remote is not undoable, so they stay local. The manifests are just filename ->
# hash records and are rebuilt by re-running the ingest.
General Knowledge/
general_docs/
kb_manifest.json
pdf_manifest.json
__pycache__/

# Restore point for records deleted from Pinecone/storage. Holds full 3072-dim
# vectors and the original brochure images, so it is large and binary-ish -- it
# is a local safety net, not something to carry in the repo.
backups/

# Supplier brochures and the rendered page images from them. Same reasoning as
# General Knowledge/ above: these are OEM documents we were given, not ours to
# publish. .render/ is a rebuildable scratch dir (pdftoppm output) and would add
# hundreds of megabytes. catalogue_manifest.json is just resume state.
new catalogues/
.render/
catalogue_manifest.json

# Same again for the backfill: rendered pages of the OEM brochures, plus the
# cached vision output that lets a --go run reuse what the dry run already read.
.render_backfill/

# Competitor brochures gathered for the DG Tech comparison work. Same rule as
# "new catalogues/" above, for the same reason, plus one more: these are a
# competitor's documents rather than ours, and the folder is 117 MB. A git
# remote keeps that forever even if a later commit deletes the files, and the
# note at the top of this file applies -- publishing is not undoable.
# What the ingest produced from them IS committed: competitor_review.md,
# competitor_size_review.md/.json and competitor_manifest.json. The sources
# stay local.
DG TECH competetion/
